WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.799 --> 00:00:11.950
Dear Fellow Scholars, hold on to your papers,
because we have an emergency situation.

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:16.840
Everybody can make deepfakes now by recording
a voice sample, such as this one, and the

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:21.900
lips of the target subject will move as if
they themselves were saying this.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:24.300
Not bad huh?

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:29.760
And now to see what else this new method can
do, first, let’s watch this short clip of

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:35.050
a speech, and make sure to pay attention to
the fact that the louder voice is the English

00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:39.590
translator, and if you pay attention, you
can hear the chancellor’s original voice

00:00:39.590 --> 00:00:52.589
in the background too.

00:00:52.589 --> 00:00:54.780
So what is the problem here?

00:00:54.780 --> 00:01:00.030
Strictly speaking, there is no problem here,
this is just the way the speech was recorded,

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:05.000
however, what if we could recreate this video
in a way that the chancellor’s lips would

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:10.200
be synced not to her own voice, but to the
voice of the English interpreter?

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:15.280
This would give an impression as if the speech
was given in English, and the video content

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:17.010
would follow what we hear.

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:22.260
Now that sounds like something straight out
of a science fiction movie, perhaps even with

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:27.440
today’s advanced machine learning techniques,
but let’s see if it’s possible.

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:35.160
This is a state of the art technique from
last year that attempts to perform this.

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:47.381
Hmm…there are extraneous lip movements which
are the remnants of the original video, so

00:01:47.381 --> 00:01:52.520
much so that she seems to be giving two speeches
at the same time.

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:53.700
Not too convincing.

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:57.050
So, is this not possible to pull off?

00:01:57.050 --> 00:02:09.090
Well, now, hold on to your papers, let’s
see how this new paper does at the same problem.

00:02:09.090 --> 00:02:18.230
Wow, now that’s significantly better!

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:22.730
The remnants of the previous speech are still
there, but the footage is much, much more

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:23.730
convincing.

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:28.360
What’s even better is that the previous
technique was published just one year ago

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:30.530
by the same research group.

00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:34.690
Such a great leap in just one year, my goodness!

00:02:34.690 --> 00:02:36.420
So apparently, this is possible.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:43.410
But I would like to see another example, just
to make sure.

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:47.340
Checkmark.

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:53.010
So far, this is an amazing leap, but believe
it or not, this is just one of the easier

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:57.790
applications of the new model, so let’s
see what else it can do!

00:02:57.790 --> 00:03:02.990
For instance, many of us are sitting at home,
yearning for some learning materials, but

00:03:02.990 --> 00:03:07.560
the vast majority of these were recorded in
only one language.

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:22.459
What if we could redub famous lectures into
many other languages?

00:03:22.459 --> 00:03:23.800
Look at that!

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:29.310
Any lecture could be available in any language
and look as if they were originally recorded

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:33.310
in these foreign languages as long as someone
says the words.

00:03:33.310 --> 00:03:37.990
Which can also be kind of automated through
speech synthesis these days.

00:03:37.990 --> 00:03:41.709
So, it clearly works well on real characters…but.

00:03:41.709 --> 00:03:43.770
Are you thinking what I am thinking?

00:03:43.770 --> 00:03:48.660
Three, what about lip syncing animated characters?

00:03:48.660 --> 00:03:53.660
Imagine if a line has to be changed in a Disney
movie, can we synthesize new video footage

00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:57.290
without calling in the animators for a yet
another all-nighter?

00:03:57.290 --> 00:04:02.819
Let’s give it a try!

00:04:02.819 --> 00:04:03.940
Indeed we can!

00:04:03.940 --> 00:04:04.940
Loving it!

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:06.510
Let’s do one more.

00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:10.450
Four, of course, we have a lot of these meme
gifs on the internet.

00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:15.200
What about redubbing those with an arbitrary
line of our choice?

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:18.989
Yup, that is indeed also possible.

00:04:18.989 --> 00:04:20.729
Well done!

00:04:20.729 --> 00:04:25.570
And imagine that this is such a leap just
one more work down the line from the 2019

00:04:25.570 --> 00:04:31.340
paper, I can only imagine what results we
will see one more paper down the line.

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:37.029
It not only does what it does better, but
it can also be applied to a multitude of problems.

00:04:37.029 --> 00:04:38.879
What a time to be alive!

00:04:38.879 --> 00:04:43.610
When we look under the hood, we see that the
two key components that enable this wizardry

00:04:43.610 --> 00:04:45.800
are here and here.

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:48.220
So what does this mean exactly?

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:53.199
It means that we jointly improve the quality
of the lip syncing, and the visual quality

00:04:53.199 --> 00:04:54.409
of the video.

00:04:54.409 --> 00:04:59.419
These two modules curate the results offered
by the main generator neural network, and

00:04:59.419 --> 00:05:04.419
reject solutions that don’t have enough
detail or don’t match the speech that we

00:05:04.419 --> 00:05:09.089
hear, and thereby they steer it towards much
higher-quality solutions.

00:05:09.089 --> 00:05:14.509
If we continue this training process for 29
hours for the lip-sync discriminator, we get

00:05:14.509 --> 00:05:16.110
these incredible results.

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:22.139
Now, let’s have a quick look at the user
study, and, humans appear to almost never

00:05:22.139 --> 00:05:25.279
prefer the older method compared to this one.

00:05:25.279 --> 00:05:27.059
I tend to agree.

00:05:27.059 --> 00:05:30.919
If you consider these forgeries to be deepfakes,
then… there you go!

00:05:30.919 --> 00:05:35.720
Useful deepfakes that can potentially help
people around the world stranded at home to

00:05:35.720 --> 00:05:38.430
study and improve themselves.

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:57.120
Imagine what good this could do!

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:31.900
Well done!

00:06:31.900 --> 00:06:35.349
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

